      "page_title": "API Test Pyramid and Contract Tests",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q41. What types of API tests should you write?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Write a ==test pyramid==: many fast unit tests, fewer integration and contract tests, a handful of end-to-end tests.",
            "Unit tests cover handlers and validation logic with mocks.",
            "Integration tests hit a real DB and check status codes, bodies and headers.",
            "Add negative cases: bad input, expired token, 404, 409.",
            "Run load and security tests before launch, not after."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Test",
            "Scope",
            "Speed"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Unit",
              "One function",
              "ms"
            ],
            [
              "Integration",
              "API + real DB",
              "seconds"
            ],
            [
              "Contract",
              "Consumer vs provider",
              "seconds"
            ],
            [
              "End-to-end",
              "Whole user flow",
              "minutes"
            ],
            [
              "Load",
              "Throughput, p99",
              "scheduled"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def test_duplicate_user(client):",
            "    client.post('/users', u)",
            "    r = client.post('/users', u)",
            "    assert r.status_code == 409",
            "    assert 'error' in r.json()"
          ],
          "note": "negative case: assert status and body"
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q42. What is contract testing and why use it?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "A ==contract test== checks a provider API still returns what consumers expect, without deploying both together.",
            "Consumers publish paths, fields and types; provider CI replays them, failing the build on a break.",
            "Catches a **removed** or renamed field before release.",
            "Tools: Pact, Spring Cloud Contract, OpenAPI diff."
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "consumer expects:",
            "  GET /users/42 -> 200",
            "  body: { id: int, name: str }",
            "provider CI replays it, must pass"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "hero": "shield-check",
      "aside": "trust, then verify",
      "subtitle": "Test it, then watch it"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "RED Metrics and Distributed Tracing",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q43. Which metrics should you monitor for an API?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Track ==RED== per endpoint: Rate, Errors, Duration; add saturation for backing resources.",
            "Latency as percentiles (p50, p95, p99), never just the average.",
            "Split errors into 4xx and **5xx**; alert on SLO burn, not every blip.",
            "Pair metrics with structured logs and traces."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Signal",
            "Example",
            "Alert when"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Rate",
              "req/sec",
              "sudden drop"
            ],
            [
              "Errors",
              "5xx %",
              "above 1%"
            ],
            [
              "Duration",
              "p99 latency",
              "above SLO"
            ],
            [
              "Saturation",
              "CPU, DB pool",
              "above 80%"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q44. How do you trace one request across microservices?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Use ==distributed tracing==: the edge assigns one trace ID and every service forwards it.",
            "W3C `traceparent` header carries trace ID and parent span ID; each service records its own timed span.",
            "Put the trace ID in every log line to join logs with traces.",
            "Instrument with OpenTelemetry; view in Jaeger or Tempo."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "Gateway",
                "sub": "creates trace-id",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "Orders",
                "sub": "span 1",
                "ink": "green"
              },
              {
                "text": "Payments",
                "sub": "span 2",
                "ink": "orange"
              },
              {
                "text": "Collector",
                "sub": "stitches trace",
                "ink": "purple"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Log hygiene",
          "text": "Log trace ID, status and latency per request, but never tokens, passwords or card numbers; mask PII."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Key takeaways",
          "text": "Test in layers, guard the service contract, watch RED metrics, follow one trace ID end to end."
        }
      ],
