    {
      "page_title": "Horizontal Scaling and Slow Endpoints",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q45. How do you scale an API horizontally?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Run **many identical instances** behind a ==load balancer==; add more as traffic grows.",
            "Keep instances ==stateless==: sessions, uploads and cache live outside the process.",
            "Move shared state to Redis, a database or object storage.",
            "Autoscale on CPU or request rate; the database is the usual next bottleneck."
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "Client -> LB -> API x N",
            "API -> Redis (sessions)",
            "API -> DB primary + replicas"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q46. How do you find and fix a slow API endpoint?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "**Measure first**: find the slow span with tracing and p95/p99 latency, not averages.",
            "Usual culprits: missing index, N+1 queries, slow downstream call, big payload.",
            "Fix with an index, caching, pagination, or async work for slow jobs.",
            "Re-measure after each change to confirm the gain."
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "EXPLAIN ANALYZE",
            "SELECT * FROM orders",
            "WHERE user_id = 42;",
            "-- Seq Scan => add an index"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Symptom",
            "Likely cause",
            "Fix"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "High DB time",
              "No index",
              "Add index"
            ],
            [
              "Many tiny queries",
              "N+1",
              "Batch or join"
            ],
            [
              "Slow third party",
              "Blocking call",
              "Timeout, cache"
            ],
            [
              "Huge response",
              "No paging",
              "Paginate, trim"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q47. What is the N+1 problem in APIs and how do you avoid it?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "One query loads a list, then **N more queries** fetch each item's related data.",
            "It looks fine in dev and collapses with real data: 100 orders means 101 queries.",
            "Avoid it with a ==JOIN==, eager loading, or one batched `IN (...)` query.",
            "In GraphQL, use a DataLoader to batch per-request lookups."
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "-- N+1: one query per order",
            "SELECT * FROM users WHERE id=?;",
            "-- Fixed: one batched query",
            "SELECT * FROM users",
            "WHERE id IN (1,2,3);"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "hero": "server",
      "aside": "measure first!",
      "subtitle": "Scale, Speed, Bulk"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Bulk Endpoints and Partial Failures",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q48. How do you design bulk endpoints and report partial failures?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Accept an ==array of items== in one request and cap the batch size (say 100).",
            "Process each item independently and return a **per-item result**.",
            "Return `207 Multi-Status` for mixed outcomes, or 200 with a results array.",
            "Make items idempotent with client IDs so a failed batch can be retried safely."
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "POST /users/bulk",
            "{\"results\":[",
            "{\"i\":0,\"status\":201},",
            "{\"i\":1,\"status\":409,",
            "\"error\":\"duplicate\"}]}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Bulk limits",
          "text": "Reject oversized batches with `413`, and hand huge imports to a 202 job instead of one request."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Key takeaways",
          "text": "Stay stateless to scale out, measure before tuning, batch to kill N+1, and report bulk results per item."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "server",
      "subtitle": "Scale, Speed, Bulk"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Designing a Double-Charge-Proof Payments API",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q49. How would you design a payments API that never double-charges?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Make every charge ==idempotent== and let the **database** be the final guard against a duplicate.",
            "Client sends a unique `Idempotency-Key` per payment attempt; the server stores key and result.",
            "A retry with the same key returns the saved response, never a second charge.",
            "Wrap charge and ledger write in one transaction with a UNIQUE key constraint.",
            "Use a state machine (created, authorized, captured) and confirm by webhook, not by timeout."
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "POST /v1/payments",
            "Idempotency-Key: 7f3a-91c2",
            "{\"amount\":4999,\"currency\":\"INR\"}",
            "// retry, same key: 200 + same id",
            "// same key, new body: 422"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "Client",
                "sub": "key 7f3a",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "Key lookup",
                "sub": "seen it?",
                "ink": "orange"
              },
              {
                "text": "Charge once",
                "sub": "in one txn",
                "ink": "green"
              },
              {
                "text": "Save result",
                "sub": "replay on retry",
                "ink": "purple"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Failure",
            "Safe handling"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Timeout, no reply",
              "Retry, same key"
            ],
            [
              "Double click",
              "Key dedupes it"
            ],
            [
              "Crash after charge",
              "Reconcile via webhook"
            ],
            [
              "Same key, new body",
              "Reject with 422"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Key storage",
          "text": "Hash the request body with the key to catch misuse, and expire stored keys after about 24 hours."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Store money as integer minor units, never floats",
            "Verify webhook signatures and dedupe by event ID",
            "Log every state change for audit and refunds"
          ],
          "style": "checkbox",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        }
